{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking approach to reducing penalties for controlled substances will end this weekend, as new legislation restores criminal charges for possessing limited quantities of powerful drugs. The updated measure, known as HB4002, allows authorities to either prosecute individuals for drug possession—now classified as a petty offense with potential short-term confinement—or offer them help through structured programs addressing behavioral health. Users can avoid financial penalties by completing a designated treatment path, though the law does not require jurisdictions to establish these alternatives. Criticism arises from concerns that the shift may lead to inconsistent enforcement across regions, as local governments decide how—or if—to implement support services.\n\nPolice will have discretion in handling cases, but accessing diversion programs comes with strict conditions, excluding those with outstanding charges, violent histories, or unstable medical needs. Officers must first verify eligibility by contacting a county hotline before connecting individuals with mobile teams tasked with providing immediate assistance. However, delays in outreach could still result in detention, as law enforcement has no obligation to wait for extended periods. Portland’s chief noted the narrow qualifications for alternative interventions, emphasizing that only those meeting specific criteria may avoid traditional penalties.\n\nSupporters argue the law grants flexibility for regions to tailor solutions based on available resources, while detractors warn of potential disparities in how policies are applied. Questions persist over whether the transition will disrupt ongoing efforts to address substance use as a health issue rather than a criminal one.\n\n***\nP.P.S.